HYPOTHESIS (final, data-grounded)

In the PCA of the twelve forms-of-capital indicators sampled at hundred-year slices
(37 centennial points, 4100-500 BCE, standardized), the symbolic-capital components
house size, daggers, and hatchets/swords behave as follows.

On PC1 (32.4% of variance) the three symbolic components ALL load with the same sign
and comparable moderate magnitude on the same pole (house size -0.17, daggers -0.23,
hatchets/swords -0.28), together with the wealth/monumental indicators (copper+gold
-0.37, depots -0.33, monument size -0.24, monument count -0.15) and opposite to the
pottery/agricultural pole (pottery form +0.41, pottery decoration -0.39). They
therefore behave as a coherent, internally consistent "symbolic / prestige capital"
axis on PC1 rather than a scattered set.

On PC2 (16.4% of variance) they do NOT form a tight cluster: they spread out into an
ordered gradient. In the primary (raw) analysis house size sits at the most negative
extreme (loading -0.37), daggers are intermediate (-0.20), and hatchets/swords lie
closest to zero (-0.14), i.e., the ordering house size < daggers < hatchets/swords.
This graded spread sits within a broad margin of the temporal PC2 scores (the 37
centennial scores span roughly -3.5 to +2.5, std ~1.4), so house size stands apart as
the most distinctive symbolic marker on PC2 while daggers and hatchets/swords occupy
moderate, close positions near the centre; the relative order of the latter two is the
least stable part of the pattern (their PC2 loadings swap in the interpolated-binned
robustness check, within ~0.05).

Declared assumptions: (1) the 12 available Z indicators are the PCA input; (2) the
`hundred-year time slices' are the 37 raw centennial points (4100-500 BCE), with the
interpolated 100-yr binned series used only as a robustness check; (3) PCA loading
signs are convention-dependent, so conclusions rely on same-pole vs opposite-pole
relations and on relative positions, not on absolute signs.
